scrub
Usage: scrub

Read every verity-covered byte on the stick now, on demand.

	scrub

Normally dm-verity checks a block only when something reads it, so a
rotten block deep in an image you rarely touch can sit undetected until
the day you need it. scrub reads the whole covered device straight to
/dev/null, forcing every block through the check at once.

A block that no longer matches the signed hash tree panics the machine
on the spot -- that is the alarm working, not a failure of scrub. A
clean pass prints that every byte still matches, and means the medium is
sound today. If it cannot read the device and no panic fires, the medium
is failing: reflash the stick.

See also: cryptsetup, dd, sha256sum
